# Design Blocks (Blocks network agent)

Before building a new page, screen, or component — or whenever choosing
colors, fonts, or layout — get direction from the `design_blocks` agent via
the blocks-network MCP server:

1. Call the `send_task` MCP tool: agent `design_blocks`, text input `brief`
   = JSON `{goal, vibe, framework, count}` or a plain sentence describing
   what you are about to build and how it should feel.

2. Poll `get_task` until complete. You get `comps` (three rendered comps,
   winner ringed — SHOW THIS to the user), `blueprint` (THE PAGE:
   section-by-section layout with exact sizes/overlaps/rotations),
   `theme_css` + `motion_css` + `motion_js` (styling and micro-animation
   kit), `stickers` (floating SVG components), `kit`, `hero`/`og` PNGs.

3. Gate: read `kit.provenance`. If `compositionSource` is
   `template-fallback`, `qualityGate.ok` is false, or `curation` is null,
   stop and re-brief with a sharper goal (page type, what the user looks
   at, what must be on screen) instead of building a fallback.

4. The comp is a DIRECTION from a mechanical compositor, not a finished
   page. Take its skeleton, hierarchy, palette roles, imagery treatment
   and type pairing; do not copy clipped headings, placeholder charts or
   bar charts standing in for lists. Derive a system first — 8px spacing,
   a type scale (screen title 28-36px, panel title 18-20px, body 15-16px,
   labels 11-12px uppercase tracked, tabular numbers), tokens for every
   colour with one accent at ~10%, hairline borders, two elevation
   levels — then build to `kit.provenance.curation.pageType`: an app
   screen is a shell + subject + same-anatomy panels + pinned markers +
   real states; a landing page is a thesis hero + blueprint sections.
   Link theme, then motion css, then motion js (defer); save hero/og into
   public/ and keep the hero credit visible. Real copy, never lorem.

5. Review at 1440 and 390 before saying done: nothing clipped, no text on
   text, no empty panel, skeleton matches the comp, one accent, titles on
   one line, first viewport full and scannable in three seconds.

6. Never: a centered gradient-card hero, three identical boxes in a row,
   framework-default fonts, emoji as icons, ad-hoc keyframes, or building
   a fallback tile as if it were a design. Mention which references were
   matched and why (the kit's curation reasons) so the user can steer.
